// @test: mutable-capture closures escape through a struct field and a Vec element
// @description: The two storing shapes the #384 record measured as silently
//   wrong (they compiled, ran and printed a raw frame address where an Int was
//   expected). With checkpoint (b)(1) both compile to a heap cell owned by the
//   closure environment, so the mutation made inside the closure survives the
//   defining frame AND is visible on the next call. Exact values are asserted,
//   not just "it does not crash".
//   LeakSanitizer note: both shapes are clean. The Vec-element shape used to
//   inherit a pre-existing leak (`Vec<fn ...>` element environments were never
//   released when the Vec was reclaimed, issue #439); that half of #439 is fixed,
//   so the boxed cell the environment owns is freed with it.
// @category: lambda, closure, mutable, escape, struct, vec, ownership
// @expect-output: field1=11 field2=12 vec0=4
// @expect-return: 0

struct Holder {
    f<fn() -> Int>
}

makeHolder()<Holder> -> {
    n<Int> = 10
    return Holder { f = || -> {
        n = n + 1
        return n
    } }
}

makeVec()<Vec<fn() -> Int>> -> {
    n<Int> = 3
    v<Vec<fn() -> Int>> = Vec()
    v.push(|| -> {
        n = n + 1
        return n
    })
    return v
}

main()<Int> -> {
    h<Holder> = makeHolder()
    g<fn() -> Int> = h.f
    a<Int> = g()
    b<Int> = g()

    v<Vec<fn() -> Int>> = makeVec()
    e<fn() -> Int> = v.get(0)
    c<Int> = e()

    println("field1=" + a.to_string() + " field2=" + b.to_string() +
            " vec0=" + c.to_string())
    return 0
}
